1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,379
Most of us met the determinant in
school, as a formula: a d minus b c.

2
00:00:05,829 --> 00:00:10,801
We used it to solve equations, to find
areas, to check whether a matrix can be

3
00:00:10,801 --> 00:00:11,247
undone.

4
00:00:11,697 --> 00:00:15,857
But to me, the most surprising place
a determinant shows up is counting.

5
00:00:16,907 --> 00:00:20,854
Counting? Ask a child, and it's
easy: one, two, cha-cha-cha!

6
00:00:21,704 --> 00:00:27,129
Ask Leslie Valiant, and he'd disagree. He
showed that counting can be brutally hard.

7
00:00:27,129 --> 00:00:31,133
Even counting the perfect matchings
of a graph is #P-complete.

8
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:36,827
And yet, in some beautiful cases, a single
determinant does the counting for us:

9
00:00:36,827 --> 00:00:41,546
routes that never cross, tilings of
a hexagon, dominoes on a chessboard.

10
00:00:41,996 --> 00:00:46,476
Let's try to see how that actually works,
starting with something small: two ants.

11
00:00:48,093 --> 00:00:50,205
Meet two ants. They live on a grid.

12
00:00:50,655 --> 00:00:55,199
Every step goes one dot right, or
one dot up. Never left, never down.

13
00:00:55,649 --> 00:00:59,980
The blue ant, ant one, lives here.
The orange ant, ant two, lives there.

14
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:05,318
There's one small problem. They had a
fight. Now neither will set foot on any dot

15
00:01:05,318 --> 00:01:09,240
the other one walks on. Not even
later: ants leave a scent trail.

16
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:14,071
So their two routes can't touch, anywhere.
How many pairs of routes get both ants

17
00:01:14,071 --> 00:01:14,341
home?

18
00:01:14,791 --> 00:01:16,882
Maybe pause, and try to find one.

19
00:01:20,027 --> 00:01:23,462
Here's one. Ant one goes up
first, ant two goes right first.

20
00:01:23,912 --> 00:01:25,960
They never meet. Peace in our time.

21
00:01:27,593 --> 00:01:32,521
To find them all, the honest way: each
ant has six routes, so thirty-six pairs.

22
00:01:32,971 --> 00:01:37,945
Check them one by one. This pair never
meets. This one meets. So does this one. And

23
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:38,304
so on.

24
00:01:38,754 --> 00:01:40,205
Twenty pairs never meet.

25
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:48,557
Now something that looks completely
unrelated. Count the routes from each ant to

26
00:01:48,557 --> 00:01:51,761
each home, and put those four
numbers in a little square.

27
00:01:52,211 --> 00:01:56,179
Take the determinant: six times
six, minus four times four.

28
00:01:56,629 --> 00:01:57,461
Twenty.

29
00:01:59,111 --> 00:02:02,973
The same twenty. Coincidence?
Hardly. Let's find out why.

30
00:02:07,957 --> 00:02:12,288
First, those four numbers. How many
ways can ant one get home? Take a guess.

31
00:02:14,238 --> 00:02:19,787
Let's list them. Right, right, up, up.
Right, up, right, up. Right, up, up, right.

32
00:02:19,787 --> 00:02:24,456
Up, right, right, up. Up, right,
up, right. And up, up, right, right.

33
00:02:24,906 --> 00:02:25,674
Six.

34
00:02:26,124 --> 00:02:31,327
Notice: every route is four steps, two
right and two up. That's forced: home is two

35
00:02:31,327 --> 00:02:33,207
columns right and two rows up.

36
00:02:34,808 --> 00:02:38,456
So a route is just a choice of which
two of the four steps go right.

37
00:02:38,906 --> 00:02:40,570
Four choose two: six.

38
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:45,857
The same works for any start and home: a
steps right and b steps up gives a plus b

39
00:02:45,857 --> 00:02:46,801
choose a routes.

40
00:02:47,251 --> 00:02:52,515
Ant one to its own home: six. To the other
home: four. Ant two to the first home:

41
00:02:52,515 --> 00:02:55,635
four. To its own: six.
That's our little square.

42
00:02:57,268 --> 00:03:02,138
Here's the thing that bugged me when I
first saw this. Six times six counts every

43
00:03:02,138 --> 00:03:06,827
way to send each ant to its own home,
including the pairs that crash into each

44
00:03:06,827 --> 00:03:07,188
other.

45
00:03:07,638 --> 00:03:09,323
Twenty good, sixteen bad.

46
00:03:09,773 --> 00:03:14,230
Four times four counts something stranger:
each ant walking to the other one's

47
00:03:14,230 --> 00:03:14,573
house.

48
00:03:15,023 --> 00:03:20,221
So the determinant is good, plus bad,
minus swapped. For that to equal the good

49
00:03:20,221 --> 00:03:25,419
ones, the swapped pairs must cancel the
bad ones exactly. Not roughly. Exactly.

50
00:03:25,419 --> 00:03:26,735
Let's match them up.

51
00:03:28,368 --> 00:03:29,541
Take a bad pair.

52
00:03:29,991 --> 00:03:34,706
Walk along ant one's route, and stop at
the first dot that ant two also visits.

53
00:03:35,156 --> 00:03:40,033
From that dot on, swap their tails. Ant
one keeps its start and takes ant two's

54
00:03:40,033 --> 00:03:42,132
ending; ant two does the opposite.

55
00:03:42,582 --> 00:03:47,147
Look where they end: each at the other's
house. A bad pair became a swapped pair.

56
00:03:48,766 --> 00:03:50,131
And it undoes itself.

57
00:03:50,581 --> 00:03:55,151
Nothing before that dot changed, so it's
still the first meeting point, and swapping

58
00:03:55,151 --> 00:03:56,619
there puts everything back.

59
00:03:57,069 --> 00:04:02,103
Do it twice and nothing happened. Mathematicians
call that an involution. Like most

60
00:04:02,103 --> 00:04:03,255
committee meetings.

61
00:04:03,705 --> 00:04:07,481
So bad pairs and swapped pairs
that meet come in perfect couples.

62
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,303
One worry left: could a swapped pair
avoid meeting, and have no partner?

63
00:04:13,753 --> 00:04:19,495
Sweep a diagonal across the grid. Every
step moves an ant onto the next diagonal, so

64
00:04:19,495 --> 00:04:23,118
on each diagonal, each ant
stands on exactly one dot.

65
00:04:23,568 --> 00:04:28,463
Track the gap between their columns. At
the start, ant one is one column to the

66
00:04:28,463 --> 00:04:28,773
left.

67
00:04:29,223 --> 00:04:31,463
At the end, it's one column to the right.

68
00:04:31,913 --> 00:04:35,817
And from one diagonal to the next,
the gap changes by at most one.

69
00:04:36,267 --> 00:04:41,233
So somewhere it is zero: they share a dot.
That's the intermediate value theorem, in

70
00:04:41,233 --> 00:04:42,475
its most humble form.

71
00:04:44,098 --> 00:04:49,026
So every bad pair has exactly one swapped
partner, and every swapped pair has

72
00:04:49,026 --> 00:04:50,562
exactly one bad partner.

73
00:04:51,012 --> 00:04:56,289
In the determinant they cancel. The minus
sign isn't throwing things away at random.

74
00:04:56,289 --> 00:04:58,927
It removes an exact copy of the bad pairs.

75
00:04:59,377 --> 00:05:02,897
What survives is exactly the
twenty pairs that never meet.

76
00:05:04,347 --> 00:05:08,507
A bijective proof: the only kind
a combinatorialist fully trusts.

77
00:05:10,930 --> 00:05:15,448
You might be thinking: surely that's a
fluke of this little grid. Move the second

78
00:05:15,448 --> 00:05:19,186
house around, and each time, brute
force and the determinant agree.

79
00:05:19,636 --> 00:05:24,172
As long as the order stays the same: ant
one's house up and to the left of ant

80
00:05:24,172 --> 00:05:24,521
two's.

81
00:05:24,971 --> 00:05:30,050
Swap the order and there are no peaceful
routes at all. The determinant notices: it

82
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:33,355
counts the swapped pairing
instead, with a minus sign.

83
00:05:34,997 --> 00:05:38,090
Add a third ant, with its
own start and its own house.

84
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:43,106
A three-by-three table: fifty. Brute
force over all the triples: fifty.

85
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:49,536
Here's the general statement, on our three
ants. Take a one-way network with some

86
00:05:49,536 --> 00:05:54,342
starts and some ends, and count the paths
from each start to each end. That's the

87
00:05:54,342 --> 00:05:54,757
matrix.

88
00:05:55,207 --> 00:06:00,379
Expand the determinant. Each term picks
an end for every start, a pairing, with a

89
00:06:00,379 --> 00:06:04,977
plus or a minus sign, and counts every
family of paths for that pairing.

90
00:06:05,427 --> 00:06:11,045
Here: two hundred and sixteen, minus
ninety-six, minus ninety-six, minus six, plus

91
00:06:11,045 --> 00:06:12,553
sixteen, plus sixteen.

92
00:06:13,003 --> 00:06:18,033
Of the two hundred and sixteen, only fifty
never touch. And when the starts and ends

93
00:06:18,033 --> 00:06:21,387
are lined up in order, a
crossed pairing always touches.

94
00:06:21,837 --> 00:06:27,179
Now the tail swap from before pairs every
touching family with one of opposite sign:

95
00:06:27,179 --> 00:06:32,457
one hundred ninety-eight against one hundred
ninety-eight. Mathematicians call this

96
00:06:32,457 --> 00:06:35,319
a sign-reversing
involution. They all cancel.

97
00:06:35,769 --> 00:06:40,739
What's left is exactly the fifty families
that never touch. And that is true for any

98
00:06:40,739 --> 00:06:41,508
such network.

99
00:06:41,958 --> 00:06:48,700
This is the Lindström–Gessel–Viennot lemma.
Lindström found it in 1973; Gessel and

100
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:53,798
Viennot rediscovered it in 1985
and showed how much it can do.

101
00:06:54,248 --> 00:06:59,184
Physicists call walkers like ours vicious
walkers. Given that our ants had a fight,

102
00:06:59,184 --> 00:07:00,136
that seems fair.

103
00:07:00,586 --> 00:07:06,511
Brute force over n ants explodes. A
determinant takes about n cubed steps, or n to

104
00:07:06,511 --> 00:07:09,546
the omega with fast matrix multiplication.

105
00:07:09,996 --> 00:07:16,572
And omega keeps falling. Strassen broke
three in 1969, and this month it reached

106
00:07:16,572 --> 00:07:23,230
nine quarters. Proved by an AI model at
OpenAI, checked in Lean, and explained to

107
00:07:23,230 --> 00:07:27,916
the rest of us by Amir Shpilka.
Division of labour, 2026.

108
00:07:29,563 --> 00:07:34,875
So what can it count? The grid doesn't
matter. Any network of one-way roads with no

109
00:07:34,875 --> 00:07:37,883
loops: right, up, diagonal,
some roads missing.

110
00:07:38,333 --> 00:07:42,258
Count the routes from every start to
every end, and take the determinant.

111
00:07:42,708 --> 00:07:47,252
You get the number of ways for everyone
to travel without ever meeting. Here it's

112
00:07:47,252 --> 00:07:48,767
forty-two. Of course it is.

113
00:07:49,217 --> 00:07:54,195
So any puzzle that is secretly a family
of non-touching paths, a determinant can

114
00:07:54,195 --> 00:07:56,684
count. And these puzzles are everywhere.

115
00:07:58,330 --> 00:08:02,426
Here's a hexagon with every side of
length two, cut into little triangles.

116
00:08:02,876 --> 00:08:08,028
Glue two neighbouring triangles together
and you get a tile: a lozenge. Lozenges

117
00:08:08,028 --> 00:08:09,767
come in three orientations.

118
00:08:10,217 --> 00:08:15,596
A tiling covers the hexagon exactly with
lozenges. Nothing sticking out, nothing

119
00:08:15,596 --> 00:08:16,403
overlapping.

120
00:08:16,853 --> 00:08:21,781
Here's one. Notice anything? It looks like
a stack of boxes in the corner of a room.

121
00:08:22,231 --> 00:08:26,562
It's completely flat. Your brain
is doing the 3D rendering for free.

122
00:08:27,012 --> 00:08:28,655
So how many tilings are there?

123
00:08:32,030 --> 00:08:34,334
Here's the trick. Fade out the flat tiles.

124
00:08:34,784 --> 00:08:39,887
Every other tile has two vertical edges.
Enter through one, leave through the other,

125
00:08:39,887 --> 00:08:43,168
and keep going. Two edges
on the left give two chains.

126
00:08:43,618 --> 00:08:48,794
Tiles never overlap, so the chains never
touch. And going backwards, any two chains

127
00:08:48,794 --> 00:08:51,725
that don't touch fill in
to exactly one tiling.

128
00:08:52,175 --> 00:08:57,399
Now squash the hexagon onto our square grid.
Each chain becomes a route of two steps

129
00:08:57,399 --> 00:09:01,753
right and two steps up, starting and
ending exactly where our ants do.

130
00:09:02,203 --> 00:09:07,299
So the number of tilings is twenty. The
same twenty. All this time, our ants were

131
00:09:07,299 --> 00:09:08,369
tiling a hexagon.

132
00:09:10,997 --> 00:09:16,543
Seen in 3D, a tiling is a stack of boxes
in a corner, each stack no taller than the

133
00:09:16,543 --> 00:09:17,546
ones behind it.

134
00:09:17,996 --> 00:09:22,797
A bigger box, three by three by three,
gives a bigger hexagon and three chains:

135
00:09:22,797 --> 00:09:24,439
three ants that never meet.

136
00:09:24,889 --> 00:09:28,644
A three-by-three determinant counts
them: nine hundred and eighty.

137
00:09:29,094 --> 00:09:34,836
Percy MacMahon found a formula for every
box, over a century ago. Combinatorialists

138
00:09:34,836 --> 00:09:36,774
call these plane partitions.

139
00:09:38,397 --> 00:09:43,446
Another disguise. A mountain range: each
step goes up or down, and it never dips

140
00:09:43,446 --> 00:09:44,456
below sea level.

141
00:09:44,906 --> 00:09:49,407
How many ways can you draw two ranges,
one inside the other, without touching?

142
00:09:49,857 --> 00:09:54,936
A single range is counted by the Catalan
numbers. Put four of them in a two-by-two

143
00:09:54,936 --> 00:09:56,918
table: the determinant is three.

144
00:09:57,368 --> 00:09:59,459
And here they are. Exactly three.

145
00:10:01,097 --> 00:10:06,481
And one more, a beauty. This is an Aztec
diamond: rows of two, four, six squares,

146
00:10:06,481 --> 00:10:07,412
and back down.

147
00:10:07,862 --> 00:10:12,963
Tile it with dominoes. The smallest one
has two tilings. The next has eight. The

148
00:10:12,963 --> 00:10:14,048
next, sixty-four.

149
00:10:14,498 --> 00:10:15,650
Guess the next one.

150
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:22,850
One thousand and twenty-four. Powers
of two, every time. That's suspicious.

151
00:10:24,497 --> 00:10:29,338
Colour the board like a chessboard, and
there are four kinds of domino. Give each

152
00:10:29,338 --> 00:10:32,924
kind a job: a flat step, a step
up, a step down, or nothing.

153
00:10:33,374 --> 00:10:38,813
Now the dominoes link up into paths, from
the left side to the right. Dominoes never

154
00:10:38,813 --> 00:10:43,928
overlap, so the paths never touch, and
every such family comes from exactly one

155
00:10:43,928 --> 00:10:44,382
tiling.

156
00:10:44,832 --> 00:10:50,040
So once again: count routes, take the
determinant. Twelve times two minus four times

157
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:50,784
four. Eight.

158
00:10:51,234 --> 00:10:53,154
One size up: sixty-four.

159
00:10:53,604 --> 00:10:59,036
In general, order n gives two to the n
times n plus one over two. A determinant,

160
00:10:59,036 --> 00:11:01,006
hiding in a pile of dominoes.

161
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:07,851
Now pick one tiling at random, every tiling
equally likely, on a big diamond, and

162
00:11:07,851 --> 00:11:08,625
let it grow.

163
00:11:09,075 --> 00:11:10,461
This is order sixty.

164
00:11:10,911 --> 00:11:15,701
The corners freeze into brick walls. The
middle is chaos. And the boundary between

165
00:11:15,701 --> 00:11:17,162
them is a perfect circle.

166
00:11:17,612 --> 00:11:21,409
That's the picture we started with.
It's called the arctic circle theorem.

167
00:11:24,030 --> 00:11:28,409
Back to the chessboard from the start.
Dominoes aren't paths, but there's a

168
00:11:28,409 --> 00:11:32,905
determinant trick here too. Shrink it
to a two-by-three board: three tilings.

169
00:11:33,355 --> 00:11:38,594
A domino joins a black square to a white
neighbour. Write a one wherever they touch.

170
00:11:38,594 --> 00:11:43,209
Each tiling picks one entry in every
row and every column: one term of the

171
00:11:43,209 --> 00:11:43,957
determinant.

172
00:11:44,407 --> 00:11:48,653
If determinants count pairings, this
should give three. It gives minus one.

173
00:11:49,103 --> 00:11:53,648
The terms come with signs: plus for
this one, minus for these two. Here the

174
00:11:53,648 --> 00:11:55,588
cancellation eats the good ones.

175
00:11:57,230 --> 00:12:03,432
In 1961 the physicist Pieter Kasteleyn
had an idea: if the signs don't cancel the

176
00:12:03,432 --> 00:12:05,806
way you want, change the signs.

177
00:12:06,256 --> 00:12:11,745
Put a minus sign on this one edge. Only the
all-vertical tiling uses it, so only its

178
00:12:11,745 --> 00:12:12,464
sign flips.

179
00:12:12,914 --> 00:12:18,423
Now every tiling carries the same sign.
The determinant is minus three. Ignore the

180
00:12:18,423 --> 00:12:19,229
sign: three.

181
00:12:20,863 --> 00:12:25,833
On a four-by-four board, the plain
determinant says there are zero tilings. Very

182
00:12:25,833 --> 00:12:27,199
confident, very wrong.

183
00:12:27,649 --> 00:12:32,784
Put minus signs on the vertical edges of
every other column, and the determinant

184
00:12:32,784 --> 00:12:34,838
gives thirty-six. Exactly right.

185
00:12:36,463 --> 00:12:41,387
Now the full chessboard: twelve million,
nine hundred eighty-eight thousand, eight

186
00:12:41,387 --> 00:12:44,570
hundred sixteen. The five-month
count, in an instant.

187
00:12:45,020 --> 00:12:50,109
The rule behind it: every little loop of
four edges carries an odd number of minus

188
00:12:50,109 --> 00:12:50,481
signs.

189
00:12:50,931 --> 00:12:56,214
And it's not really about chessboards.
Squares are vertices, dominoes are edges, and

190
00:12:56,214 --> 00:12:58,163
a tiling is a perfect matching.

191
00:12:58,613 --> 00:13:03,960
Kasteleyn, Temperley and Fisher made this
work for any graph you can draw without

192
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:09,504
crossings: the FKT algorithm counts perfect
matchings of planar graphs in polynomial

193
00:13:09,504 --> 00:13:09,834
time.

194
00:13:10,284 --> 00:13:15,310
When a physicist needs to count twelve
million tilings, they don't count. They take

195
00:13:15,310 --> 00:13:18,519
a determinant, and then let
the board go to infinity.

196
00:13:20,163 --> 00:13:25,858
Let's step back. Ants that never meet.
Tilings of a hexagon. Boxes in a corner.

197
00:13:25,858 --> 00:13:29,102
Mountain ranges. Aztec
diamonds. Chessboards.

198
00:13:29,552 --> 00:13:31,664
All counted by one determinant each.

199
00:13:33,296 --> 00:13:38,171
And it's the same trick every time. Count
everything, good and bad, each with a

200
00:13:38,171 --> 00:13:38,480
sign.

201
00:13:38,930 --> 00:13:43,889
The bad ones pair up with opposite signs,
and cancel. What's left is exactly what

202
00:13:43,889 --> 00:13:44,562
you wanted.

203
00:13:45,012 --> 00:13:51,054
For a computer scientist, that's remarkable.
Counting is usually hopeless: counting

204
00:13:51,054 --> 00:13:57,023
perfect matchings of a general graph is
sharp-P complete. That's Valiant, in 1979.

205
00:13:57,473 --> 00:14:02,573
But if your puzzle turns into non-touching
paths, or a matching on a planar graph,

206
00:14:02,573 --> 00:14:05,558
it becomes one determinant,
and polynomial time.

207
00:14:06,008 --> 00:14:09,315
Determinants can count,
because minus signs can cancel.

208
00:14:12,463 --> 00:14:17,559
As for our ants: they can finally go home
in peace. Twenty ways home, and they never

209
00:14:17,559 --> 00:14:18,287
had to meet.

210
00:14:20,237 --> 00:14:21,496
Thanks for watching.

